  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nWarm and dry weather conditions will persist into the day Friday, \nbut a more active pattern this weekend into next week will bring \nback chances for showers and thunderstorms to our forecast area. A \nweak upper-level shortwave passing to our northwest today has \ngenerated light rain across western Arkansas and some cloud cover \nacross the region, but the impact of the ridge over the southeast \nhas maintained dry and mostly clear conditions across our CWA. \nToday's mid-70s high temps and near-50 low temps are near normal for \nlate October.\n\nAn upper-level shortwave pivoting through a deep longwave trough \nover the western CONUS will eject into the Southern Plains Friday. \nLatest model guidance show a negatively-tilted shortwave with \nminimal surface reflection as the wave lifts from the Southern High \nPlains into the Midwest this weekend. It will be deep and slow \nenough that surface dewpoints near 65 degrees will advect northward \ninto our forecast area in advance of the wave. While some of the \nrain chances Friday night into Saturday may be more of a top-down \nprocess driven by the mid-level dynamics, a moistening and warming \nboundary layer will gradually support more surface-based and \nelevated convection heading into Saturday and Sunday. GFS MLCAPE \nvalues approach 1000 J/kg across the far southern parts of our area \nwhich is a notable return of instability that has been lacking in \nrecent weeks. Correspondingly, thunder chances and QPF values have \nincreased along with POPs. Light rain could be ongoing early \nSaturday morning, with chances for heavier rain and thunderstorms \nincreasing into the day Saturday. Across the CWA, there are now \nmedium (50-60%) chances for rain during the Saturday and Saturday \nnight periods. Trick-or-Treat plans should account for the \npossibility of rain or thunder in the area. The shortwave axis will \nbe slow to depart, with 20-40% POPs lingering into early Sunday. \n\nA brief rebound in shortwave ridging after the weekend system will \nlower rain chances until Tuesday, but can't rule out some rain in \nthat time. The West Coast trough should break down over the weekend \nand the overall pattern become more progressive. A low pressure \nsystem deepening out of this trough is then expected to dig and spin \nover the Southern Plains early in the week. Timing and spacing of \nthe features is uncertain for that kind of pattern this far out, but \nit will likely result in a stronger frontal system crossing the \nregion. More robust guidance solutions support a cold front squall \nline scenario on Tuesday. Explicit instability values are low this \nfar in advance, but greater moistening (dew points over 65) and flow \nresulting in better wind shear may bring a chance for more organized \nthunderstorm development or even severe weather to our area on \nTuesday. /NF/",
